  "title": "Neha Bora's Peace Bond Refusal and Legal Challenge",
  "summary": "Neha Bora, president of the All India Students' Association (AISA), refused to sign a peace bond under a police notice, questioning the rationale behind the requirement. The notice, issued under Section 126 of the Indian Penal Code, mandates her to appear before a special executive magistrate (SEM) and provide a personal bond of Rs 10,000. She also challenged the notice's legality, arguing that it constitutes a curb on dissent.",
  "facts": [
    {
      "statement": "Neha Bora refuses to sign a peace bond under a police notice issued on October 17.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    },
    {
      "statement": "The notice requires her to appear before a special executive magistrate (SEM) to prove her right to keep peace for a period of inquiry.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    },
    {
      "statement": "Neha Bora questioned the legality of the notice, citing it as a curb on dissent.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    },
    {
      "statement": "The police notice is based on Section 126 of the Indian Penal Code and applies to curbing dissent under Section 126 of the Indian Penal Code.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    }
